    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2027077228580741177",
      "url": "https://x.com/ibukun_tayo/status/2027077228580741177",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/ibukun_tayo/status/2027077228580741177",
      "text": "Tricycle ambulances may look “local,” but they are a global, practical innovation saving lives in hard to reach communities.\n\nCountries like India, Bangladesh, Uganda, Sierra Leone, Ghana, and even parts of Philippines to mention a few, use tricycle or motorcycle ambulances, especially in rural or densely populated areas.\n\nWhy are they used???:\n\n1. Narrow roads conventional ambulances can’t access...\n2. Heavy traffic congestion... Common Sense.\n3. Flood-prone or rough terrains.\n4. Lower cost of purchase and maintenance (Equipment functionality)\n\nLet's define clearly the purpose of an ambulance (any type you know): An ambulance 🚑 is not just a vehicle, or an helicopter 🚁 it is a mobile emergency unit designed to...\n1. Provide rapid response to emergencies victim...\n2. Deliver first aid and basic life support.\n3. Stabilize patients before reaching the hospital....\n4. Safely transport the sick or injured, from one location to another.\n\nFacts for the critics, if you wish to learn and unlearn.\n\nThe goal of emergency medicine is speed + access, not the size of the vehicle!. A delayed “big ambulance” can cost more lives than a smaller one that arrives early(know this and know peace ✌️) Many global health programs recommend adapted transport systems for low resource settings. Tricycle ambulances complement, however does not replace, conventional ambulances, I guess this was where you got me wrong.\n\nInnovation is not a downgrade, it is adaptation.\nIf it has an oxygen, bleeding control, and can support a patient to definitive care faster, it is doing the job. Why are you screaming at me? For what?",
      "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
